--TEST--
Both `Core\Topic` members take the name as a sink, so neither a subscription nor its undoing can be chosen by the input the program is answering — `rule:core-classes/topic`, `rule:observability/default-series`
--ARGS--
room:someone-elses
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 4 states the rule and names the reason: a name derived from user input is
// how one tenant subscribes to another's stream. It is the same refusal
// `rule:security/metric-label-refuses-tainted` applies to a metric label, and it is a *compile* error rather than a
// check inside the member, so a program cannot reach the bus with an unchecked
// name at all -- there is no run in which the wrong subscription happened once.
//
// The two are asked together because the interesting property is that they
// *agree*: a member that refused a tainted name on the way in and accepted one
// on the way out would let a request cancel a subscription it was never able to
// make, which is the same tenant-crossing read backwards.
foreach (Core\Cli::arguments() as tainted string $word) {
    Core\Topic::subscribe($word);
    Core\Topic::unsubscribe($word);
    echo "unreachable\n";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:13:27
   |
13 |     Core\Topic::subscribe($word);
   |                           ^^^^^ this is `tainted string`

error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:14:29
   |
14 |     Core\Topic::unsubscribe($word);
   |                             ^^^^^ this is `tainted string`

